    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's get back to the science.\n\nBlood meal and bone meal are both high-nitrogen fertilizers, but they can have varying effects on soil pH. Blood meal, being a high-potassium fertilizer, tends to be more pH-stable, but it can still raise the pH slightly due to the potassium content. Bone meal, on the other hand, is a more pH-sensitive fertilizer, as it contains calcium and phosphorus, which can increase the soil pH.\n\nIn your case, with a soil pH of 6.2, adding blood meal or bone meal could potentially increase the pH by 0.1-0.3 units, depending on the specific product and application rate. This might not seem like a lot, but it can still affect the availability of nutrients for your kale.\n\nFor example, if your soil pH is 6.2, adding 1-2% blood meal or bone meal (by weight) could raise the pH to around 6.3-6.5. This might not be a significant change, but it can impact the availability of nutrients like iron, manganese, and copper, which are more readily available at lower pH levels.",
